#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096608 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "65b4e8e7-e558-50e7-a1ec-37a6eff77e87" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10b22226-96da-5ac3-837f-85c6d962014a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477761} {:answer/id #uuid "23b112a7-45d9-59f5-b11b-4a043ad38f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477806} {:answer/id #uuid "d040139f-5db2-5449-b5ff-e7cc5e152405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.644)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478148} {:answer/id #uuid "dccbee6a-dd05-5649-ad87-d2d6be5453e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.644)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478168} {:answer/id #uuid "ff0972a5-aa0a-506b-9f2d-6cb313e5a657" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-1.107)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478244}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23b112a7-45d9-59f5-b11b-4a043ad38f89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477806} :answer-field/id #uuid "efe9231d-0800-5ee8-8509-d4d9ca580e2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478361}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41ce9e80-1405-5642-831b-08af7169731b" :question/math-academy-id "q-107575" :question/problem "$3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & ≈0.927\\,\\text{rad}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places.\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(3)^{2}+(4)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡ϕ+{sin}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =9+16 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x≈5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-0.927)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478465 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63a826e3-1f4a-56c0-a4f6-8617344946df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477940}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63a826e3-1f4a-56c0-a4f6-8617344946df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477940} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c664a42-d4ca-5edc-9480-a51aeced73f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478278}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fb921c8c-f3ea-56c4-a4fb-c6ad860350a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-274555" :question/problem "Given that the expression $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$\n\n$ϕ=$ \n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The sum formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\sqrt{3} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =60\\,{\\,}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed6854a-cf74-591e-8fe2-00619854ab94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477853} {:answer/id #uuid "454d32d4-5e30-5d47-9068-bb3220f00149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186477866} {:answer/id #uuid "8a107828-920e-532b-946a-234e69c84613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478015} {:answer/id #uuid "9788048e-f8b7-5586-af01-ee2107752378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478031} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a20e78-4df7-5e41-99a4-c960fe582b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478150}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a107828-920e-532b-946a-234e69c84613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478015} :answer-field/id #uuid "78daaf88-e999-5da1-8bf1-f099b8191645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478308}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5e4ccfa-82ff-5c85-afb9-eb5972468216" :question/math-academy-id "q-30579" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\n \n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ ϕ & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the amplitude $R.$ Squaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & ={(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}^{2}+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {R}^{2} & =1 \\\\ R & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$\n\nFinally, we can write the initial expression in amplitude-phase form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{3})\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186478452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096609 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f12d8028-2520-5706-ba49-d67935036af6" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Amplitude-Phase Form for Cosine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c19d84af-2e7c-52ec-9fb2-1c4d02dfc257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c19d84af-2e7c-52ec-9fb2-1c4d02dfc257" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478113} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a184adc-97c3-5fd4-aa16-2ab651c506af" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186478287}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc2a00c1-69ff-5ca9-838c-e411473b3c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-327994" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ)$ with $R>0,$ what is the value of $R?$\n\n$R=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for cosine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=R\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring the first and the second equations of the system and adding the results together, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)}^{2}+{(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)}^{2} & =(4)^{2}+(3)^{2} \\\\ {R}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡ϕ+{cos}^{2}⁡ϕ) & =16+9 \\\\ {R}^{2} & =25 \\\\ R & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we only consider the positive root since $R>0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335847 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096606 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74c40813-d2aa-5eff-b5dc-69a2f916f865" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Phase for the Amplitude-Phase Form for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b5b735d-63dc-59e6-b41c-77003c0e40a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333398} {:answer/id #uuid "57318f06-cd50-578b-ae0b-794fe204ec64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333782} {:answer/id #uuid "575d47bf-3cbd-57ee-92d6-60684fb5f9d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333788} {:answer/id #uuid "688f9863-4f33-5417-9de0-c09ccf5fcb8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333949} {:answer/id #uuid "a0da0bdd-42a6-5ec1-a897-867603b5eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0da0bdd-42a6-5ec1-a897-867603b5eeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334401} :answer-field/id #uuid "44d64f22-bb75-503b-b5b4-1ad2bd5bcbcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335369}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e0c19689-c107-5415-8074-e35dadeefb3e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65582" :question/problem "Given that the expression $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ can be written as $R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ)$ with ${0}^{∘}<ϕ<{90}^{∘}$ and $R>0$, what is the value of $ϕ?$" :question/worked-solution "The difference formula for sine gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ) & =R(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ) \\\\  & =R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by writing the given expression in the amplitude-phase form\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=R\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-ϕ),\n$$\n\nwe get the following identity:\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)=(R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-(R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)$ and $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x),$ we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=4 \\\\ R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{R\\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ}{R\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ} & =\\frac{10}{4} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡ϕ & =\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ ϕ & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
